<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">North Korea</h1><p class="st-meta">1948 CE – present · capital Pyongyang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Communist one-party state on the northern half of the Korean peninsula, founded in 1948 under Kim Il Sung and led since by his son and grandson.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Following the Japanese surrender in 1945 the northern half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> was occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. A <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/soviet-civil-administration">Soviet Civil Administration</a> was set up in August 1945, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a> became chairman of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/provisional-peoples-committee-for-north-korea">Provisional People's Committee for North Korea</a> in February 1946. The Democratic People's Republic of Korea was established on 9 September 1948, with Kim as premier.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>North Korea invaded the South on 25 June 1950; the war ended with an armistice on 27 July 1953, and an estimated 12-15% of North Korea's population died. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a> purged rival factions in 1956 and promoted the ideology of Juche, while staying closely aligned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Soviet aid ended in 1991 and the economy almost collapsed. A famine from 1994 to 1998 killed between 240,000 and 420,000 people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-jong-il">Kim Jong Il</a>, who instituted the Songun policy, held power from 1994 to 2011.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Kim Jong Un succeeded his father on 17 December 2011; the country has continued to develop nuclear weapons. On 30 December 2023 Kim announced that unification was no longer possible, and in May 2026 the constitution was amended to describe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> as a hostile state. The Workers' Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> is the sole ruling party, and Amnesty International has expressed concerns about religious persecution.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/north-korea.jpg" alt="North Korea" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Korea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1948 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pyongyang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kim Il Sung, Kim Jong Il, Kim Jong Un</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Officially atheist; most people irreligious, with Chondoism, Korean shamanism and Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Korean</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Democratic People's Republic of Korea, DPRK</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1987&amp;focus=north-korea" aria-label="North Korea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7795 779 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where North Korea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8115 1035L8115 1035M8115 1035L8115 1035"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8115 1035h0M8115 1035h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8115 1035h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8115 1035h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pyongyang. <a href="/atlas?year=1987&amp;focus=north-korea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
